title: When Code Gets Cheap, What Do You Sell?
date: 2026-06-07
author: Florian Buetow
scope: Software-delivery consultancies in the age of AI-assisted construction
question: "As construction commoditizes, where does a consultancy's scarce value sit?"

stages: Genesis, Custom, Product, Commodity
legend

// Who we serve
anchor Client

// What the client buys, and how they pay
Verified Outcome : II.5 >> III.2
Commercial Model : III.0
Working Software : II.7 >> III.1

// The scarce, still-novel value: the new craft lives on the LEFT
Software Factory : I.8 >> II.6
Orchestration : I.6 >> II.6
Automated Evals : I.7 >> II.7
Senior Judgment : II.0 >> II.4

// The industrialized, commoditizing layer the factory runs on lives on the RIGHT
AI Coding Agents : III.5 >> IV.0
Foundation Models : III.8 >> IV.3
Cloud Compute : IV.6 (outsource)

// Value chain
Client -> Verified Outcome
Client -> Commercial Model
Verified Outcome -> Working Software
Working Software -> Software Factory
Software Factory -> Orchestration
Software Factory -> Automated Evals
Software Factory -> AI Coding Agents
Orchestration -> Senior Judgment
Automated Evals -> Senior Judgment
AI Coding Agents -> Foundation Models
Foundation Models -> Cloud Compute

// Pricing mechanisms placed by their CURRENT maturity, not by where strategy wants to go:
// the billable hour is the commodity; bespoke outcome pricing is the novel, risky one.
pipeline Commercial Model {
  Outcome-Based : I.8
  Shared-Savings Bridge : II.4
  Fixed Price : III.5
  Billable Hour : IV.2
}

// Team alignment to evolution phase
group The New Craft {
  team: pioneer
  Software Factory
  Orchestration
  Automated Evals
  Senior Judgment
}

group Industrialized Layer {
  team: town-planner
  AI Coding Agents
  Foundation Models
  Cloud Compute
}

// Climate
signal commoditization on AI Coding Agents
signal commoditization on Foundation Models
signal co-evolution on Software Factory
signal co-evolution on Orchestration

// Manoeuvres: strategic intent climbs LEFT, toward the scarce value
gameplay ILC "Build the software factory once, reuse it across clients" on Software Factory
gameplay strangler-fig "Progressively replaced by outcome pricing: a bridge, not a big bang" on Billable Hour

// Strategic notes
note "Not something you buy: a lifecycle rewrite where agents construct and humans design what they operate within" on Software Factory
note "Stays scarce: deciding what to build and proving it was built right; only the codifiable parts drift right" on Senior Judgment
note "Boolean pass/fail gives way to scoring the fraction of runs that satisfy intent" on Automated Evals
note "For bespoke software, paying on a verified result is the rare, novel end, not yet the norm" on Outcome-Based
